<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad al-Nasir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1199–<a href="/years/1213">1213</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as al-Nasir fled</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad al-Nasir was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> from 1199 to 1213, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur">Yaqub al-Mansur</a>. He faced a coalition of Christian Iberian kingdoms and was decisively defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212, after which he fled. The defeat marked the beginning of the end of Almohad power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-nasir.jpg" alt="Muhammad al-Nasir" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LouisAragon · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_coin_of_Almohad_ruler_Abu_%27Abd_Allah_Muhammad_(r._1199-1213).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Muhammad al-Nasir, born around 1182, was the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> caliph. Christians of his time called him Miramamolín, and he took the regnal title al-Nasir li-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>. His father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur">Abu Yusuf Yaqub al-Mansur</a>, died on 25 January 1199, and al-Nasir was proclaimed caliph that same day. He inherited an empire showing signs of instability. His father’s victories against the Christians in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> had relieved him for a time of serious threats there, and he was able to concentrate on defeating the attempts of the Banu Ghaniya to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>.</p><p>To deal with problems elsewhere in the empire he appointed Abu Muhammad Abd al-Wahid as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, and so, without intending it, began the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafsid-dynasty">Hafsid dynasty</a> there, which lasted until 1574. In October 1205 Abd al-Wahid led 4,000 cavalry and crushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-ghaniya">Yahya ibn Ghaniya</a> at Jabal Tajra, and in January 1206 he obtained the surrender of al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdia">Mahdia</a>, whose governor rallied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> cause.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> then turned back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> at the request of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. A Christian coalition defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212. He died the following year and was succeeded by his young son Yusuf al-Mustansir, born of a Christian slave named Qamar.</p><p>A story told by the chronicler Matthew Paris, writing two decades after the events, claims that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, under pressure after a quarrel with Innocent III that placed England under an interdict, sent envoys to al-Nasir offering to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and make England a Muslim state in return for help. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> was said to be so disgusted by the plea that he sent the envoys away. Historians doubt the story because no other contemporary evidence supports it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad al-Nasir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Al-Mansur's death in 1199 and the succession of his son <b>Muhammad al-Nasir</b> gave the Christian kingdoms of Iberia time to consolidate and to form a coalition under papal crusading sanction.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1207, after the Almohad caliph <b>Muhammad al-Nasir</b> had driven the Banu Ghaniya, Almoravid holdouts who had overrun much of the eastern Maghreb, out of Tunis, he appointed Abu Hafs’s son Abu Muhammad Abd al-Wahid governor of Ifriqiya, roughly modern Tunisia and eastern Algeria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.02,-6.84&amp;year=1206" aria-label="Rabat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4376 931 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Muhammad al-Nasir"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4826 1201h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4826 1201h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.02,-6.84&amp;year=1206">What was here in 1206? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
